WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.970
We started the company Jan 17, 2023, right post-Chat GPT,

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:06.920
but really we were thinking

3
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.870
about all of this stuff before it came out.

4
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.420
I was working back

5
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.600
before I started the company

6
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.030
on this side project.

7
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.730
Do you know the website Wirecutter?

8
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.510
Top 10 best non-stick pans, things like that.

9
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.320
My family loves these products, right?

10
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.200
My uncle will spend 20 hours reading reviews

11
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.480
to buy a $30 pan

12
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.200
just because it's like a hobby,

13
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:26.960
it's like a thing to do.

14
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.500
So I was working on a side project

15
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.170
back in 2022

16
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.600
of hyper-personalized AI-written Wirecutter articles

17
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.980
that would aggregate up

18
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.430
all of the reviews on the internet.

19
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.510
You can't tell on a podcast,

20
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.290
but I'm very tall, I'm six foot five.

21
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.310
It's hard to find sleeves that are long enough.

22
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.040
But that information is actually captured

23
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.670
in people who write reviews who say,

24
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.060
oh my god,

25
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.310
I bought this shirt for my husband,

26
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.160
he's so tall, shirts never fit him,

27
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.170
but this one happened to fit.

28
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.080
And that information is locked up semantically there,

29
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:53.780
even though it doesn't say

30
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.450
that the sleeves are especially long.

31
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.320
And so I had built this product, again,

32
00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.410
as a side project,

33
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.390
really pre Chat GPT.

34
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.850
It's like GPT-3 Codex, an 8,000 token context window.

35
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.880
And it took 15 minutes to run

36
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.620
and create a thing.

37
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:08.600
It cost $50

38
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.610
to do one of these little listicles.

39
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.190
And honestly, it was really bad.

40
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.380
But there was a glimmer there

41
00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.820
of just this amazing ability

42
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.900
to take huge amounts of unstructured

43
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.060
semantically rich data

44
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.220
and pull some sort of interesting insight

45
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:21.890
out of it.

46
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.920
And I just fell in love

47
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:24.780
with that problem space

48
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.760
that I was looking around,

49
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:26.770
wanted to start a company.

50
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.190
Got connected to my now co-founder Austin,

51
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.130
and he was working at Ramp, doing some growth automation stuff.

52
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.360
And I was chatting with him,

53
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.360
and a lot of the stuff

54
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.040
he was thinking about

55
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.000
was a B2B version

56
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.020
of what I was thinking about,

57
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.110
except instead of for buyers, it was for sellers.

58
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.650
And I paused and reflected.

59
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:44.870
I was a classic engineer.

60
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.290
I was like, yuck, sales.

61
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:47.760
Before I started the company,

62
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.550
before I had done a bunch of work in sales,

63
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:50.860
I thought the job to be done

64
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.200
on a salesperson was rhetoric.

65
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.430
I thought it was,

66
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.010
how do I convince you to buy my product?

67
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.390
And that's totally not the case at all.

68
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.340
Actually, what a great salesperson says is they say,

69
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.100
the job to be done in sales

70
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.740
is to find people in companies

71
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.530
that have a problem that my product uniquely solves.

72
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:08.900
And then it doesn't really matter

73
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:09.620
what you say to them.

74
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.170
Yes, you have to communicate credibly,

75
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.710
and communicate effectively, and be believable.

76
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.350
But at the end of the day,

77
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.480
in kind of a funny way,

78
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.300
the economy is just us solving

79
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.730
each other's problems for money.

80
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.320
And when you reframe it as a search problem,

81
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.900
just a bunch of really interesting stuff

82
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:23.730
falls out of it.

83
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:24.680
It's this search problem

84
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.440
that historically was run by people

85
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.250
in this sort of organization.

86
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.990
The system was a system of people.

87
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.210
But with LLMs, and AI,

88
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.390
and really high-scale data and software engineering,

89
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.940
you can make a much, much better system

90
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.000
that gives you repeatability,

91
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.370
observability, scalability.
